<section id="faq-section" class="content-section" style="background-color: #ffffff;">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions &ndash; IT Demand Section 156 in Mumbai</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Get answers about jurisdictional authority, response time, disagreement options, non-payment consequences, stay of demand, common causes, legacy demands, and interest for Mumbai taxpayers.</p>
                    @include('partials.bigin-form', [
                        'variant'  => 'compact',
                        'service'  => 'IT Demand Section 156',
                        'city'     => 'Mumbai',
                    ])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the jurisdictional authority for Mumbai?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Mumbai taxpayers fall under various CIT ranges depending on PAN and ward. CPC Bengaluru handles Section 143(1) processing. Appeals to CIT(A)/JCIT(A) over the AO. ITAT Mumbai Bench for second appeals. Patron coordinates with specific jurisdictional AO for each Mumbai taxpayer's demand.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How much time to respond?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>30 days from service of demand notice. AO can shorten with JCIT approval. Taxpayer can apply for extension or instalment before deadline. If no response: demand becomes final, adjusted against refund, or recovery initiated. Patron ensures timely response for all Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What if I disagree with the demand?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Three options: (1) Rectification Section 154 if computational/clerical error. (2) Appeal to CIT(A) Section 246A within 30 days if substantive dispute. (3) Portal response selecting Disagree with reasons and documents. Patron recommends correct remedy for each Mumbai taxpayer.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens if I don't pay within 30 days?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Classified as assessee in default (Section 220). Interest 1% per month on unpaid. Recovery: bank attachment, salary attachment, property attachment, arrest in extreme cases. Patron files timely responses to prevent default classification for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can demand be stayed pending appeal?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Section 220(6): AO can grant stay. CBDT: if appeal filed and 20% paid, AO should normally stay balance. AO has discretion and may require more. If refused: approach CIT(A) or ITAT. Patron files stay citing CBDT circulars for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Most common reason for Mumbai IT demands?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>TDS credit mismatch. CPC doesn't give credit because Form 26AS/AIS doesn't match. Causes: employer delayed TDS deposit, wrong PAN, bank TDS missing. Solution: rectification Section 154, not payment. Patron reconciles TDS and files rectification.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can old outstanding demands be cleared?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Legacy demands: payment already made (link challan), appeal decided in favour (upload order), duplicate demands (request deletion). Patron identifies resolution for each and clears from portal for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is interest on unpaid demand?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 220(2): simple interest 1% per month on unpaid from expiry of 30-day period. In addition to interest in demand (Sections 234A/B/C). Accumulates until paid. Patron computes total exposure and advises optimal payment timing for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Section 156 ka notice aaya hai &ndash; kya kare?</strong> E-filing portal pe jaao, demand notice download karo. Form 26AS/AIS se match karo. TDS credit nahi mila toh rectification file karo Section 154 mein. AO ne income add kiya toh appeal file karo CIT(A) ke paas 30 din mein. Demand sahi hai toh 30 din mein pay karo. Patron 2-3 din mein analysis karke best strategy batata hai.</p>
                <p><strong>Bank account freeze ho gaya?</strong> Emergency stay application file karo AO ke paas. Appeal file karo. 20% pay karo. CBDT circular cite karo. AO nahi sunta toh CIT(A) ya ITAT jaao. Patron same day file karta hai Mumbai mein.</p>
                <p><strong>Purane demand portal pe dikh rahe hain?</strong> Check karo: payment ho chuki hai (challan upload karo), appeal order favour mein hai (order upload karo), ya duplicate hai. Patron legacy demands portal se clear karta hai.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

